#!/bin/sh
# A commit that changes a decision says so. Every commit touching fitness/ carries a line
#
#   Decision: (adds|reconfigures|retires) fitness/<file> because ...
#
# written by the maintainer in their own words: fitness/ holds the decisions about this repository's code, and
# a decision that changes silently is the one thing RFC 0027 exists to prevent. The line is the one exception
# to CLAUDE.md's "no generated trailers" -- no tool adds it.
#
# `git commit --no-verify` bypasses this, as it bypasses any hook; the `decision` job in CI is what binds.
# `npm install` switches the hook on, through the `prepare` script that sets core.hooksPath.

message_file="$1"
[ -f "$message_file" ] || exit 0

# What this commit stages. A merge or a rebase stages nothing, so there is nothing to judge.
staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
echo "$staged" | grep -q '^fitness/' || exit 0

# A comment line is not part of the message git keeps.
if grep -v '^#' "$message_file" | grep -Eq '^Decision: (adds|reconfigures|retires) fitness/'; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
refused: this commit changes a decision, and does not say so.

fitness/ holds the decisions about this repository's code. A commit that touches it carries one line, in
your own words, saying which of adds, reconfigures or retires, which file, and why:

  Decision: adds fitness/the-engine-imports-nothing.fitness.ts because the engine's orthogonality is the
  rule I would least like to lose quietly.

The files this commit stages under fitness/:
MSG
echo "$staged" | grep '^fitness/' | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

A fitness function that bites is a design signal: the edit usually goes to the code, not to fitness/.
If you meant to change a decision, write the line. `git commit --no-verify` skips this hook; CI will not.
MSG
exit 1
